1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,980
The United States is and always has been a melting pot of cultures, customs, ideas, and yes, food.

2
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:15,300
One of the things that I love most about food is that it can and always does evolve.

3
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:19,900
Coming up with new inventions, new innovations, that's an American tradition.

4
00:00:20,220 --> 00:00:26,740
And we've been revolutionizing the world of food just like every other part of our society since our founding days.

5
00:00:26,741 --> 00:00:29,540
In fact, there are a lot of iconic foods

6
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:31,940
you probably associate with other countries

7
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,580
that were actually invented right here in the U.S. of A.

8
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:37,680
Like English muffins?

9
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:39,580
Yeah, that's ours.

10
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,280
The Cuban sandwich?

11
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:43,820
That's an American innovation.

12
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,100
Even salad dressings like Russian, French, and ranch

13
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,400
invented here, too.

14
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:52,200
American innovations also include

15
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,300
the first chocolate that didn't melt in summer heat,

16
00:00:55,301 --> 00:00:59,800
the first condensed soup, and so much more.

17
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,300
Today, we're getting a look at American inventions

18
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:04,100
and innovations up close.

19
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,340
We'll go behind the curtain at world-famous Dippin' Dots,

20
00:01:07,440 --> 00:01:10,020
where we'll see how a process originally created

21
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:13,580
for feeding cattle gave us a brilliant reinvention

22
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:14,520
of ice cream.

23
00:01:14,620 --> 00:01:18,520
Plus, we'll see how Blue Apron is changing the grocery gate

24
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:21,720
by using innovative machines on the assembly line.

25
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:24,300
You won't believe how they're shipping millions

26
00:01:24,301 --> 00:01:27,100
of specially designed packages every month

27
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,440
across the country.

28
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:32,500
And we'll see behind the curtain at the Jelly Bean Company

29
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:36,040
that blew up the jelly bean world by figuring out

30
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:39,040
how to pack hundreds of crazy new flavors

31
00:01:39,140 --> 00:01:41,760
into the center of their beans.

32
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:46,060
This is Modern Marvel's Inventions and Innovations.

33
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:56,300
What is innovation?

34
00:01:56,301 --> 00:01:58,300
To me, it's that spirit of asking, is there a better way?

35
00:01:58,301 --> 00:02:00,300
How can this improve?

36
00:02:00,301 --> 00:02:03,300
Maybe it's improving a process or making something more efficient,

37
00:02:03,301 --> 00:02:06,300
or maybe it's creating something completely new.

38
00:02:06,301 --> 00:02:10,300
The biggest and most beloved innovations have changed the world as we know it,

39
00:02:10,301 --> 00:02:15,300
like the light bulb, the automobile, and the supermarket.

40
00:02:15,301 --> 00:02:19,300
Today, I'm with the CEO of the Supermarket.

41
00:02:19,301 --> 00:02:28,340
Today, I'm at one company that is reinventing the grocery process that had been in place

42
00:02:28,341 --> 00:02:29,980
for 100 years.

43
00:02:29,981 --> 00:02:34,760
From the idea of the company itself to the processes and machinery they've implemented,

44
00:02:34,761 --> 00:02:40,700
the whole thing is one big innovation, Blue Apron.

45
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:47,800
Founded in 2012, Blue Apron is one of the leaders in this $5 billion meal kit industry,

46
00:02:47,801 --> 00:02:52,640
almost half a billion dollars in revenue last year alone.

47
00:02:52,641 --> 00:02:57,940
I'm currently inside their 495,000 square foot,

48
00:02:57,941 --> 00:03:00,520
custom-built, state-of-the-art facility.

49
00:03:00,521 --> 00:03:05,480
And it is very big, bigger than a New York City block.

50
00:03:05,481 --> 00:03:09,560
And today, we're going to find out how they take 125 truck

51
00:03:09,561 --> 00:03:12,660
loads of ingredients, portion them, organize them,

52
00:03:12,661 --> 00:03:16,800
and pack and ship hundreds of thousands of meals

53
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:18,460
every single week.

54
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:26,180
Grocery stores are responsible for about 43 billion pounds of food waste in the U.S.

55
00:03:26,340 --> 00:03:28,480
That waste costs you money.

56
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:33,000
Blue Apron's developed a host of innovative approaches to getting food to your table

57
00:03:33,001 --> 00:03:37,220
that minimize waste and maximize efficiency.

58
00:03:37,620 --> 00:03:42,080
That saves you money at the same time it's setting new standards for the industry.

59
00:03:42,081 --> 00:03:45,520
Their innovations, including some of the most cutting-edge

60
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:48,580
machinery I have ever seen, are changing

61
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:52,160
our entire relationship with food, how we cook it,

62
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:55,360
how we package it, and how we shop for it.

63
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:58,160
And I want to figure out how they do it.

64
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:02,360
Each Blue Apron box contains a recipe for a meal,

65
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:05,800
as well as a set of perfectly portioned ingredients

66
00:04:05,900 --> 00:04:07,740
to make the meal with.

67
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:11,380
The menu is constantly changing to keep things interesting,

68
00:04:11,381 --> 00:04:13,880
as well as focusing on what's in season.

69
00:04:13,980 --> 00:04:17,380
Before we see the process of how they work their magic,

70
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:19,680
I want to see some of the finished meals.

71
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:23,120
It starts with a meal prep box.

72
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:27,860
Meal prep is an old idea busy Americans have long employed.

73
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:31,260
Cook on one day, but prep all the meals

74
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,840
for your entire hectic work week.

75
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:38,000
But Blue Apron is reinventing the whole idea of meal prep

76
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:39,780
with their new box.

77
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:43,180
You take one day to prep and cook four different meals

78
00:04:43,220 --> 00:04:45,620
and then reheat them through the week.

79
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:48,660
So let's see what's for dinner.

80
00:04:48,680 --> 00:04:51,760
John Adler, a former chef in my hometown of Brooklyn,

81
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,360
is Blue Apron's VP of culinary and physical products.

82
00:04:55,400 --> 00:04:59,760
It is his job to make sure all of Blue Apron's meals

83
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:01,260
are delicious.

84
00:05:01,300 --> 00:05:04,340
So what you have here is chicken, pork chorizo meatballs,

85
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:06,980
and then some mashed sweet potatoes and broccoli.

86
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,780
I'm just curious with something like the mashed potatoes,

87
00:05:09,781 --> 00:05:12,740
If you can tell what's different about our mashed potatoes

88
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:14,660
than maybe most mashed sweet potatoes.

89
00:05:14,661 --> 00:05:15,660
OK, you got it.

90
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,300
Mm.

91
00:05:21,301 --> 00:05:23,000
I love the consistency of it.

92
00:05:23,001 --> 00:05:25,160
But now I'm going to name something for you

93
00:05:25,161 --> 00:05:28,300
that you're going to say, no, that's a dessert.

94
00:05:28,301 --> 00:05:29,140
Mascarpone cheese.

95
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:30,260
Mascarpone.

96
00:05:30,261 --> 00:05:32,640
Mascarpone is how you get that creaminess.

97
00:05:32,641 --> 00:05:36,180
What we do is we start to think about what's a plus one.

98
00:05:36,181 --> 00:05:39,440
What are different things that we can add to the experience?

99
00:05:39,441 --> 00:05:42,580
where someone is going to feel like it was memorable and exciting.

100
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:47,080
Think about all the different ingredients

101
00:05:47,081 --> 00:05:49,160
that come in a Blue Apron box.

102
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:51,580
Meats like duck and chorizo.

103
00:05:52,180 --> 00:05:54,460
Produce like chickpeas and bok choy.

104
00:05:54,461 --> 00:05:57,060
Not to mention sauces and seasonings.

105
00:05:57,380 --> 00:06:00,800
So how does Blue Apron deliver all those different foods

106
00:06:00,801 --> 00:06:04,080
to your door in one box, fresh and ready to cook?

107
00:06:04,640 --> 00:06:07,500
Well, it's time to be Blue Apron president and CEO, Linda,

108
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:08,520
and find out.

109
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:10,340
Shall we go?

110
00:06:10,341 --> 00:06:11,240
You lead, I follow.

111
00:06:11,241 --> 00:06:12,240
All right.

112
00:06:12,241 --> 00:06:13,240
Let's do it.

113
00:06:13,241 --> 00:06:14,240
I'll hold your apron string.

114
00:06:14,241 --> 00:06:15,540
See how I did that?

115
00:06:15,541 --> 00:06:17,340
It's, oh, forget it.

116
00:06:17,341 --> 00:06:20,080
Because the menu is constantly changing,

117
00:06:20,081 --> 00:06:23,580
the machines at assembly line have to be adaptable to handling

118
00:06:23,581 --> 00:06:26,900
all the different ingredients, sauces, spices, basically

119
00:06:26,901 --> 00:06:29,920
everything and anything you can think to cook with.

120
00:06:29,921 --> 00:06:32,500
Blue Apron has a major challenge.

121
00:06:32,501 --> 00:06:35,040
How to keep all these different ingredients

122
00:06:35,041 --> 00:06:37,000
with different storage temperatures

123
00:06:37,001 --> 00:06:39,340
fresh through their entire process

124
00:06:39,440 --> 00:06:41,540
and all the way to your doorstep.

125
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,440
So we have receiving docs that actually bring in all the produce,

126
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:46,100
all the protein, et cetera.

127
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:49,280
So we have a very specific cold chain handling process

128
00:06:49,380 --> 00:06:51,580
to make sure ingredients stay at the right temperature

129
00:06:51,680 --> 00:06:52,620
for that ingredient.

130
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:53,880
OK.

131
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:56,720
So for example, tomatoes should not be as cold as, say, milk.

132
00:06:56,820 --> 00:06:57,680
You hear that?

133
00:06:57,780 --> 00:06:59,820
Hey, you don't put your tomatoes in the fridge.

134
00:06:59,920 --> 00:07:01,420
Never put your tomatoes in the fridge.

135
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:02,420
Come on, people.

136
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:04,760
It crystallizes.

137
00:07:04,761 --> 00:07:07,060
How many trucks are going to be coming in?

138
00:07:07,061 --> 00:07:07,960
I mean...

139
00:07:07,961 --> 00:07:10,960
You know, we can have anywhere from 100 to 125 a week,

140
00:07:10,961 --> 00:07:13,560
so we've got about 25 trucks coming in per day

141
00:07:13,561 --> 00:07:14,900
just for receiving.

142
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:17,840
And if I may, I just want to show one thing.

143
00:07:19,880 --> 00:07:24,700
That despite the fact that this is meal kits you can order,

144
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:26,580
you're getting actual produce.

145
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,920
This isn't like a freeze-dried, reconstituted pepper.

146
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:34,000
And here is where Blue Apron's innovative process

147
00:07:34,001 --> 00:07:37,900
offers a big advantage over a traditional grocery store.

148
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:40,600
If anybody has bought one of those peppers in the grocery store,

149
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:41,980
you know how expensive they are.

150
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:42,820
Yes.

151
00:07:42,821 --> 00:07:46,520
The reason they're expensive is because of the waste surrounding the system.

152
00:07:47,180 --> 00:07:49,680
So if a lot of those peppers are going to get thrown out,

153
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:52,000
then each individual pepper has to cost more.

154
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:55,020
We can give it to you at a much better cost

155
00:07:55,021 --> 00:07:57,820
because of the fact that we know it's not going to be wasted.

156
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:01,840
Blue Apron's approach is new,

157
00:08:01,841 --> 00:08:04,680
But the idea of inventing new ways to help busy families

158
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:08,280
get dinner on the table has a long history.

159
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:10,180
One of the first companies to do it

160
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:12,320
wasn't even aiming for the dinner table.

161
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:14,720
They were aiming for a TV tray.

162
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:17,260
The year was 1953.

163
00:08:17,360 --> 00:08:20,600
The company, Swanson, was already a successful purveyor

164
00:08:20,700 --> 00:08:21,960
of frozen poultry.

165
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:26,660
But that year, they had a whopping 260 tons

166
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:30,160
of frozen turkey left over from Thanksgiving

167
00:08:30,161 --> 00:08:32,500
and faced a dilemma in how to monetize

168
00:08:32,501 --> 00:08:34,160
their expensive surplus.

169
00:08:34,161 --> 00:08:36,340
Then, a brilliant idea.

170
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:38,040
To carve up those birds

171
00:08:38,041 --> 00:08:40,800
and add sides of stuffing and sweet potatoes

172
00:08:40,801 --> 00:08:44,800
and freeze all three in a partitioned aluminum tray

173
00:08:44,801 --> 00:08:46,640
that could be heated up at home.

174
00:08:46,641 --> 00:08:49,640
Swanson had just invented an American legend,

175
00:08:49,641 --> 00:08:51,480
the TV dinner.

176
00:08:51,481 --> 00:08:52,780
And get this.

177
00:08:52,781 --> 00:08:55,480
Some husbands even wrote angry letters

178
00:08:55,481 --> 00:08:57,180
to Swanson at the time,

179
00:08:57,181 --> 00:09:01,360
miffed about losing their fancy, full-blown, home-cooked meals.

180
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:06,520
But a TV dinner is frozen and stays frozen

181
00:09:06,521 --> 00:09:08,400
until you pick it up at the supermarket.

182
00:09:08,401 --> 00:09:11,020
A gourmet meal kit with fresh ingredients

183
00:09:11,021 --> 00:09:12,660
delivered right to your door?

184
00:09:12,661 --> 00:09:15,160
That is a new innovation entirely.

185
00:09:15,161 --> 00:09:17,000
Think about an avocado.

186
00:09:17,001 --> 00:09:19,040
You hand-test them at the supermarket

187
00:09:19,041 --> 00:09:22,080
to make sure they're in that small window

188
00:09:22,081 --> 00:09:23,740
of perfect ripeness.

189
00:09:23,741 --> 00:09:25,540
How does Blue Apron make sure

190
00:09:25,541 --> 00:09:28,380
the multitude of avocados they send out

191
00:09:28,480 --> 00:09:32,480
arrive at your doorstep within that tiny window.

192
00:09:32,580 --> 00:09:35,080
Avocados has always been a tricky ingredient for meal

193
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:37,880
kits because very hard to get that perfect ripeness.

194
00:09:37,980 --> 00:09:40,520
You know it's even harder having it stored, shipped

195
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:42,620
to a customer, and then having it be the perfect ripeness

196
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:43,800
when it gets there.

197
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:45,420
This machine here is going to help tell us

198
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:48,560
how much pressure it takes to penetrate into that fruit.

199
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:50,660
It's called a penetrometer.

200
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:52,340
So we're going to penetrate into the fruit.

201
00:09:52,440 --> 00:09:55,540
Are we allowed to say that on this family show?

202
00:09:55,541 --> 00:09:57,580
We want the ripeness of the fruit

203
00:09:57,680 --> 00:10:01,040
to be between 10 and 15 pounds per square inch.

204
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:03,640
So I'm going to place the avocado with the cut side

205
00:10:03,740 --> 00:10:05,080
right underneath our device.

206
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:08,580
Perfect.

207
00:10:08,680 --> 00:10:13,300
Avocado, it's almost like a tightrope walk.

208
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:14,400
It's like they're rocking.

209
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:16,500
You need to nail that sweet spot.

210
00:10:16,600 --> 00:10:21,040
You have to do that for one component of one recipe

211
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:24,300
in one kit that may have multiple recipes in it.

212
00:10:24,301 --> 00:10:29,800
So this is a small sample of the produce quality assurance.

213
00:10:29,801 --> 00:10:33,040
We haven't even yet entered the cold chain.

214
00:10:33,041 --> 00:10:36,840
A cold chain is a variation of the standard supply chain,

215
00:10:36,841 --> 00:10:40,280
where foods are transported from producer to consumer

216
00:10:40,281 --> 00:10:42,980
at a certain temperature to maximize their freshness.

217
00:10:42,981 --> 00:10:46,280
But at Blue Apron, they've taken it to the next level.

218
00:10:46,281 --> 00:10:49,100
Instead of the one-size-fits-all approach of either

219
00:10:49,101 --> 00:10:50,960
refrigerate or don't refrigerate,

220
00:10:50,961 --> 00:10:54,260
they've innovated and have subdivided their cold

221
00:10:54,261 --> 00:10:57,260
to include three different temperature coolers

222
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,160
along with their freezer.

223
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:01,260
And because of their innovative approach,

224
00:11:01,360 --> 00:11:05,600
their ingredients are stored at their own ideal temperature,

225
00:11:05,700 --> 00:11:09,700
locking in freshness and keeping the prices down.

226
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:12,320
Besides being immensely complicated,

227
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:16,920
Blue Apron's cold chain runs at an insane size.

228
00:11:17,020 --> 00:11:20,260
This is like the biggest, best pantry ever.

229
00:11:20,261 --> 00:11:21,260
ever.

230
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,340
When it comes to inventions and innovations,

231
00:11:26,341 --> 00:11:30,760
American candy makers have done their share and then some.

232
00:11:30,761 --> 00:11:33,800
Over 100 years ago, New Yorker Leo Hirschfield

233
00:11:33,801 --> 00:11:36,860
changed American childhood when he invented a new candy

234
00:11:36,861 --> 00:11:41,420
that combined a chocolatey taste with a taffy consistency,

235
00:11:41,421 --> 00:11:43,200
the Tootsie Roll.

236
00:11:43,201 --> 00:11:46,520
Tootsie Roll's taffy base gave them durability,

237
00:11:46,521 --> 00:11:48,620
making them the very first chocolate that

238
00:11:48,621 --> 00:11:50,960
wouldn't melt in summer heat.

239
00:11:50,961 --> 00:11:53,320
In an era before refrigerators and AC,

240
00:11:53,321 --> 00:11:55,520
that was a very big deal.

241
00:11:55,521 --> 00:11:58,600
Tootsie Rolls were also the first single-serving candy

242
00:11:58,601 --> 00:12:02,200
to be individually wrapped and sold for one cent.

243
00:12:02,201 --> 00:12:05,100
For the first time, even the poorest kids

244
00:12:05,101 --> 00:12:08,380
could pick up a penny candy at the corner store.

245
00:12:10,300 --> 00:12:12,080
One of those poor kids went on to become

246
00:12:12,081 --> 00:12:15,680
a 20th century pop culture titan, Frank Sinatra.

247
00:12:15,681 --> 00:12:18,340
Old Blue Eyes loved Tootsie Rolls so much,

248
00:12:18,341 --> 00:12:20,580
He was reportedly buried with some.

249
00:12:20,581 --> 00:12:24,480
And the iconic treat is still going strong in the 21st century,

250
00:12:24,481 --> 00:12:28,520
with 64 million Tootsie Rolls rolling off the line

251
00:12:28,521 --> 00:12:32,020
every single day.

252
00:12:32,021 --> 00:12:35,000
Here at Blue Apron, they have completely refashioned

253
00:12:35,001 --> 00:12:38,320
the traditional assembly line with innovative concepts,

254
00:12:38,321 --> 00:12:41,240
like the cold chain, which starts right here

255
00:12:41,241 --> 00:12:45,300
in this enormous room at their flagship New Jersey facility.

256
00:12:45,301 --> 00:12:49,240
This is like the biggest, best pantry ever.

257
00:12:49,241 --> 00:12:50,200
Absolutely.

258
00:12:50,201 --> 00:12:52,380
This is our cooler number one.

259
00:12:52,381 --> 00:12:53,200
OK.

260
00:12:53,201 --> 00:12:55,980
Peppers, eggplant, tomato.

261
00:12:55,981 --> 00:12:57,540
This is the right climate for them?

262
00:12:57,541 --> 00:12:58,620
Absolutely.

263
00:12:58,621 --> 00:13:01,680
You'll have three, four meals in a box.

264
00:13:01,681 --> 00:13:05,080
But again, you don't know if the customer's

265
00:13:05,081 --> 00:13:07,020
going to cook them right away, what order they're

266
00:13:07,021 --> 00:13:08,460
going to cook them in.

267
00:13:08,461 --> 00:13:12,000
So they have to be hardy enough for a whole week, correct?

268
00:13:12,001 --> 00:13:12,900
Yep.

269
00:13:12,901 --> 00:13:14,800
And by keeping them at their perfect temperature,

270
00:13:14,801 --> 00:13:16,840
you know, that 45 to 55 degree range,

271
00:13:16,841 --> 00:13:18,600
we're going to extend that shelf life.

272
00:13:18,601 --> 00:13:19,800
Absolutely.

273
00:13:19,801 --> 00:13:23,500
Blue Apron has four different cooler storage spaces,

274
00:13:23,501 --> 00:13:25,840
all designed to keep every ingredient

275
00:13:25,841 --> 00:13:28,040
within its ideal temperature range.

276
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:33,520
Back in the 80s, a microbiologist named Kirk Jones

277
00:13:33,521 --> 00:13:36,020
came up with a food innovation that also involved

278
00:13:36,021 --> 00:13:37,060
cold temperatures.

279
00:13:37,061 --> 00:13:40,060
But he was thinking just a smidge colder,

280
00:13:40,061 --> 00:13:42,920
negative 320 degrees.

281
00:13:42,921 --> 00:13:46,420
Dippin' Dots revolutionized the idea of ice cream

282
00:13:46,421 --> 00:13:49,820
when they burst on the scene in 1988.

283
00:13:49,821 --> 00:13:53,060
Jones was working at a company that used liquid nitrogen

284
00:13:53,061 --> 00:13:56,000
to freeze yogurt and to feed pellets for cattle,

285
00:13:56,001 --> 00:13:58,460
but he had a brilliant idea.

286
00:13:58,461 --> 00:14:02,240
What if you applied the same technique to ice cream?

287
00:14:02,241 --> 00:14:05,380
His revolutionary concept created a brand new product,

288
00:14:05,381 --> 00:14:10,140
super dense, ultra creamy, tiny little spheres of ice cream.

289
00:14:10,141 --> 00:14:13,320
And every Dippin' Dot you have ever tasted

290
00:14:13,321 --> 00:14:16,080
was made right here in Paducah, Kentucky.

291
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:22,020
We get about 2.8 million pounds of cream per year,

292
00:14:22,021 --> 00:14:25,020
2.8 million pounds of skim milk per year.

293
00:14:25,021 --> 00:14:27,560
We get liquid sugar and corn syrup,

294
00:14:27,561 --> 00:14:29,420
and all that goes into the process

295
00:14:29,421 --> 00:14:34,020
of making 3.5 million gallons of Dippin' Dots per year.

296
00:14:34,021 --> 00:14:38,400
That equals 87 million servings of Dippin' Dots,

297
00:14:38,401 --> 00:14:43,840
or 174 billion actual dots, each one of which begins its life

298
00:14:43,841 --> 00:14:45,960
right here in a batch tank.

299
00:14:45,961 --> 00:14:50,900
At this stage, the mix is just like any other ice cream mix.

300
00:14:50,901 --> 00:14:53,340
Once our mix is pasteurized, it'll

301
00:14:53,341 --> 00:14:55,180
go into one of these flavor vats.

302
00:14:55,181 --> 00:14:57,640
Today, we're making birthday cake.

303
00:14:57,641 --> 00:15:02,160
There's a blue, a white, a pink, and a teal.

304
00:15:02,161 --> 00:15:05,040
Each different color goes into a different vat.

305
00:15:05,041 --> 00:15:10,040
So we keep those colors separated over into our cryogenic pelletizers.

306
00:15:10,041 --> 00:15:16,040
Here is where Dippin' Dots diverges from every ice cream maker that came before it.

307
00:15:16,041 --> 00:15:21,880
Instead of using a regular freezer to freeze their ice cream mix, they use liquid nitrogen,

308
00:15:21,881 --> 00:15:24,040
a process called cryogenics.

309
00:15:24,041 --> 00:15:28,040
And no, I don't mean freezing people when they die.

310
00:15:28,041 --> 00:15:29,040
For the record, that's cryonics.

311
00:15:29,041 --> 00:15:32,040
That's the subject of a very, very different show.

312
00:15:32,041 --> 00:15:36,340
Liquid nitrogen is so incredibly cold.

313
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:40,280
320 degrees Fahrenheit below zero.

314
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:43,020
And because of that, it can freeze food,

315
00:15:43,120 --> 00:15:45,860
especially ice cream, in an instant,

316
00:15:45,960 --> 00:15:48,820
which is such an advantage because it can result in really,

317
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:51,160
really, really small ice crystals

318
00:15:51,260 --> 00:15:53,300
so that you don't detect them by the tongue,

319
00:15:53,400 --> 00:15:56,740
which is why Dippin' Dots become really popular over the years.

320
00:15:56,840 --> 00:15:58,960
You're eating spheres of ice cream

321
00:15:59,060 --> 00:16:01,100
instead of a scoop of ice cream.

322
00:16:01,101 --> 00:16:03,100
And this is where the magic happens.

323
00:16:03,101 --> 00:16:07,100
Droplets of the mix fall directly into liquid nitrogen,

324
00:16:07,101 --> 00:16:11,100
which, at a chilly 320 degrees below zero,

325
00:16:11,101 --> 00:16:14,940
freezes the dots with much smaller ice molecules

326
00:16:14,941 --> 00:16:16,440
than conventional ice cream.

327
00:16:16,441 --> 00:16:20,860
This gives Dippin' Dots their trademark smooth, creamy texture.

328
00:16:20,861 --> 00:16:23,560
The dots also look very different.

329
00:16:23,561 --> 00:16:26,560
They are tiny, smooth, individual spheres,

330
00:16:26,561 --> 00:16:30,560
as opposed to traditional ice cream, which is an emulsion.

331
00:16:30,561 --> 00:16:33,860
The dots are then collected at the bottom of the processor

332
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:36,400
and taken onto packaging.

333
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:39,240
Each bag holds one gallon of ice cream.

334
00:16:39,340 --> 00:16:42,480
There's roughly 25 servings in a gallon of ice cream.

335
00:16:42,580 --> 00:16:45,040
Each serving has about 2,000 dots.

336
00:16:45,140 --> 00:16:48,040
She's filling each bag individually.

337
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,540
From there, he's taking the bags and running it

338
00:16:50,640 --> 00:16:52,280
through a sealer.

339
00:16:52,380 --> 00:16:53,880
On the other side of the van sealer

340
00:16:53,980 --> 00:16:57,880
is our quality assurance person, and she's checking the color,

341
00:16:57,881 --> 00:17:00,880
Make sure the blend and proportion is right.

342
00:17:00,881 --> 00:17:03,880
They're packaging six of those bags to a case.

343
00:17:03,881 --> 00:17:05,880
And Dippin' Dots have to be stored in a freezer

344
00:17:05,881 --> 00:17:08,880
at negative 45 degrees.

345
00:17:08,881 --> 00:17:12,880
That is far colder than what's used for conventional ice cream.

346
00:17:12,881 --> 00:17:15,880
This is all of the different ice cream that we're storing.

347
00:17:15,881 --> 00:17:17,880
Each row's a different flavor.

348
00:17:17,881 --> 00:17:21,880
So all the different spews that we handle are in this freezer.

349
00:17:21,881 --> 00:17:23,880
The Dippin' Dots stay fresh here

350
00:17:23,881 --> 00:17:26,880
until they're ready to be shipped to kiosks in all 50 states,

351
00:17:26,881 --> 00:17:30,040
50 states and 11 countries around the world.

352
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:36,680
Some now classic candies like the Tootsie Roll

353
00:17:36,681 --> 00:17:39,040
burst on the scene as brand new inventions.

354
00:17:39,041 --> 00:17:43,000
Others like the jelly bean evolved over time.

355
00:17:43,001 --> 00:17:47,760
Jelly beans are a hybrid of two much older confections.

356
00:17:47,761 --> 00:17:50,620
They have the chewy center of Turkish delight

357
00:17:50,621 --> 00:17:53,160
combined with the shell of Jordan almonds.

358
00:17:53,161 --> 00:17:56,880
The term jelly bean first appears in 1861.

359
00:17:56,881 --> 00:18:00,780
when Boston candy maker William Schrafft encouraged customers

360
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:04,080
to send his jelly beans to Union soldiers

361
00:18:04,180 --> 00:18:05,420
during the Civil War.

362
00:18:05,520 --> 00:18:07,920
With their egg-like shape, jelly beans

363
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:11,420
soon became a staple of Easter baskets everywhere.

364
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:14,540
But they didn't change much until a candy company

365
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:17,240
called Jelly Belly came up with the idea

366
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:21,680
of a gourmet jelly bean in the 1960s.

367
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:25,580
At the time, jelly beans only came in simple flavors,

368
00:18:25,581 --> 00:18:27,240
like lemon and licorice.

369
00:18:27,241 --> 00:18:30,280
And those flavors were only added to the shell of the bean.

370
00:18:30,281 --> 00:18:33,960
Jelly Belly's big innovation was to pack the center

371
00:18:33,961 --> 00:18:37,600
of the jelly bean with a huge variety of new

372
00:18:37,601 --> 00:18:40,960
and interesting flavors.

373
00:18:40,961 --> 00:18:45,440
So the mini gourmet jelly bean that we made at that time

374
00:18:45,441 --> 00:18:47,860
was the precursor to Jelly Belly.

375
00:18:47,861 --> 00:18:52,600
And in 1976, we added in these peanut butter

376
00:18:52,601 --> 00:18:56,440
and coconut flakes and coffee and juices and purees.

377
00:18:56,441 --> 00:19:00,140
By using these flakes, juices, and purees,

378
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:04,480
Jelly Belly is able to pack big flavor into these tiny beans

379
00:19:04,481 --> 00:19:08,420
and once again, innovated a candy that had predominantly

380
00:19:08,421 --> 00:19:11,180
remained unchanged for over 100 years.

381
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:15,360
And their revolutionary flavors built Jelly Belly

382
00:19:15,361 --> 00:19:18,160
into the candy titan it is today,

383
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:21,720
beloved by everyone, from preschoolers

384
00:19:21,721 --> 00:19:23,060
to presidents.

385
00:19:23,061 --> 00:19:25,760
We sent three and a half tons of candy

386
00:19:25,761 --> 00:19:28,960
to the inauguration for President Reagan

387
00:19:28,961 --> 00:19:30,920
in red, white, and blue colors,

388
00:19:30,921 --> 00:19:32,860
which we actually had to come out with blue

389
00:19:32,861 --> 00:19:35,360
because we hadn't made a blueberry bean yet,

390
00:19:35,361 --> 00:19:38,480
which has been a top-selling flavor ever since.

391
00:19:40,780 --> 00:19:43,780
Today, the Jelly Belly factory in Fairfield, California

392
00:19:43,781 --> 00:19:46,880
cranks out an astonishing 20 million jelly beans

393
00:19:46,881 --> 00:19:48,920
every single day.

394
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:51,720
So here at Jelly Belly, we have over 150 flavors

395
00:19:51,721 --> 00:19:54,220
and they all have to start here in our kitchen

396
00:19:54,221 --> 00:19:55,720
as a cook slurry.

397
00:19:55,721 --> 00:19:57,720
So every year, we end up going through

398
00:19:57,721 --> 00:20:00,220
about 20 million pounds of sugar,

399
00:20:00,221 --> 00:20:03,560
about 6 million pounds of corn syrup.

400
00:20:03,561 --> 00:20:08,060
So the factory is about 250,000 square feet.

401
00:20:08,061 --> 00:20:11,180
And it takes between 7 to 14 days

402
00:20:11,181 --> 00:20:15,720
to make a Jelly Belly jelly bean from beginning to end.

403
00:20:15,721 --> 00:20:18,720
The first step is to make the center of the bean.

404
00:20:18,721 --> 00:20:21,720
That's real cinnamon oil that's going into the bean.

405
00:20:21,721 --> 00:20:23,720
of a Jelly Belly jelly bean.

406
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:28,360
So each kettle is going to produce about 150,000 beans.

407
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:32,060
And we're going to make about 20 million beans today.

408
00:20:32,160 --> 00:20:33,760
And now the color is going in.

409
00:20:36,060 --> 00:20:37,760
The hard work starts to pay off

410
00:20:37,860 --> 00:20:40,280
downstairs, where the cinnamon slurry

411
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:43,280
drops into these molding trays and first

412
00:20:43,380 --> 00:20:45,380
takes the shape of a jelly bean.

413
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:48,220
While the candy is a hot liquid form,

414
00:20:48,320 --> 00:20:51,220
we want to shape it to the exact dimensions

415
00:20:51,221 --> 00:20:54,160
that we want of each piece of candy.

416
00:20:54,220 --> 00:20:57,820
So we have over 1,000 beans per tray.

417
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,660
We do over 30 trays a minute.

418
00:21:00,720 --> 00:21:03,600
And we are talking about some seriously

419
00:21:03,660 --> 00:21:05,660
pampered jelly beans.

420
00:21:05,740 --> 00:21:08,640
After resting overnight, it's time to hit the spa

421
00:21:08,700 --> 00:21:12,780
for a steam bath, followed by a sugar shower.

422
00:21:12,840 --> 00:21:14,540
Yes, you heard me right.

423
00:21:14,600 --> 00:21:16,380
So the beans go through a steam bath

424
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:18,840
to get them a little moist so that they

425
00:21:18,841 --> 00:21:22,340
so that they can go through a sugar shower,

426
00:21:22,341 --> 00:21:26,840
and then go down into this elevator, up,

427
00:21:26,841 --> 00:21:30,080
and all the way down to a robot to be stacked.

428
00:21:30,081 --> 00:21:31,880
So after these beans are stacked,

429
00:21:31,881 --> 00:21:34,760
they will be resting overnight.

430
00:21:34,761 --> 00:21:37,720
It's time to add syrup, sugar, flavor, and color

431
00:21:37,721 --> 00:21:40,760
to form the shell around the center of the bean.

432
00:21:40,761 --> 00:21:43,960
Every run is a bank of eight pans.

433
00:21:43,961 --> 00:21:49,460
He's doing about 150,000 beans in each pan.

434
00:21:49,461 --> 00:21:51,800
So he's doing over a million beans.

435
00:21:51,801 --> 00:21:53,160
And now we're going to take those beans,

436
00:21:53,161 --> 00:21:55,180
now that they have a shell, and we're

437
00:21:55,181 --> 00:21:57,300
going to put a confectioner's glaze,

438
00:21:57,301 --> 00:22:01,380
and they're going to sparkle and shine.

439
00:22:01,381 --> 00:22:04,140
But not every bean will make it into a bag.

440
00:22:04,141 --> 00:22:08,880
This screen separates Jelly Bellies from belly flops.

441
00:22:08,881 --> 00:22:11,460
So when the bean is the right size,

442
00:22:11,461 --> 00:22:16,460
goes through this screen, and it works its way up.

443
00:22:16,461 --> 00:22:17,960
This is a belly flop.

444
00:22:17,961 --> 00:22:21,960
It's about five beans all stuck in there.

445
00:22:21,961 --> 00:22:25,460
So a belly flop is a Jelly Belly bean

446
00:22:25,461 --> 00:22:29,460
that is not the correct size, color, or shape.

447
00:22:29,461 --> 00:22:33,460
Only flawless beans get that famous Jelly Belly logo.

448
00:22:33,461 --> 00:22:37,460
We started to print the beans because we had imitators

449
00:22:37,461 --> 00:22:40,460
that were not the original Jelly Belly.

450
00:22:40,461 --> 00:22:44,300
So this machine runs a set of rollers

451
00:22:44,301 --> 00:22:50,360
that will put that Jelly Belly logo as it goes through.

452
00:22:50,361 --> 00:22:53,100
So now we're going to go see how we pack it into a bag.

453
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:02,860
This is probably my favorite robot in the whole place.

454
00:23:02,861 --> 00:23:04,560
These are our grab-and-go bags.

455
00:23:04,561 --> 00:23:06,700
This is the best bag out there.

456
00:23:06,701 --> 00:23:09,620
So this is the final stage.

457
00:23:09,621 --> 00:23:11,280
We've made that bean.

458
00:23:11,281 --> 00:23:13,420
It's now packed and ready to go.

459
00:23:13,421 --> 00:23:17,500
So we make about 2,000 beans per second

460
00:23:17,501 --> 00:23:22,760
and about 15 billion beans per year.

461
00:23:22,761 --> 00:23:24,560
These Jelly Bellies are finally

462
00:23:24,561 --> 00:23:26,300
ready to head out into the world,

463
00:23:26,301 --> 00:23:28,840
destined to create smiles wherever they land.

464
00:23:31,540 --> 00:23:34,980
Back at Blue Apron, it's time to hit the factory floor.

465
00:23:34,981 --> 00:23:37,540
But here, they call it the kitchen.

466
00:23:37,541 --> 00:23:40,140
That's because unlike a typical factory floor,

467
00:23:40,141 --> 00:23:43,140
which produces the same product day in and day out,

468
00:23:43,141 --> 00:23:46,420
on this factory floor, just like a kitchen,

469
00:23:46,421 --> 00:23:49,560
hundreds of different foods are prepared in a lineup

470
00:23:49,561 --> 00:23:51,780
that changes every single day.

471
00:23:51,781 --> 00:23:54,020
To do that, Blue Apron had to innovate.

472
00:23:54,021 --> 00:23:57,320
Each individual machine had to handle a wide variety

473
00:23:57,321 --> 00:24:01,040
of foods that come in a huge array of different shapes,

474
00:24:01,041 --> 00:24:02,560
sizes, and textures.

475
00:24:02,561 --> 00:24:05,000
What do we have here?

476
00:24:05,001 --> 00:24:08,000
We have scallions on the auto bagger.

477
00:24:08,001 --> 00:24:11,780
Last year alone, we sent out 23 million scallions

478
00:24:11,781 --> 00:24:13,040
to customers.

479
00:24:13,041 --> 00:24:14,180
This is insane.

480
00:24:14,181 --> 00:24:17,380
The auto bagger might be bagging scallions today,

481
00:24:17,381 --> 00:24:19,620
but what's most amazing is that tomorrow,

482
00:24:19,621 --> 00:24:22,020
it could be peanut bread or radishes.

483
00:24:22,021 --> 00:24:24,160
Basically, this machine takes the bags that

484
00:24:24,161 --> 00:24:25,880
will hold various ingredients, and they've

485
00:24:25,881 --> 00:24:28,420
been printed with the information about those ingredients.

486
00:24:28,421 --> 00:24:29,300
OK.

487
00:24:29,301 --> 00:24:31,100
Turns it right side up, and then feeds

488
00:24:31,101 --> 00:24:34,600
it through this slot where she can focus on checking

489
00:24:34,601 --> 00:24:36,100
and putting them in the bag.

490
00:24:36,101 --> 00:24:37,100
OK.

491
00:24:37,101 --> 00:24:38,600
And then when it gets to the end,

492
00:24:38,601 --> 00:24:40,600
it trims the top off and seals the bag.

493
00:24:40,601 --> 00:24:42,600
Oh, that's where it seals it.

494
00:24:42,601 --> 00:24:43,600
Exactly.

495
00:24:43,601 --> 00:24:48,600
I just also would say that she has not only done quality control

496
00:24:48,601 --> 00:24:51,600
on the scallions, but to actually be packing these things

497
00:24:51,601 --> 00:24:55,600
while they're moving, it's like hitting a moving target.

498
00:24:55,601 --> 00:24:58,600
And tomorrow, she will have to QC and pack

499
00:24:58,601 --> 00:25:03,600
a completely different item, like butter leaf lettuce or chives.

500
00:25:03,601 --> 00:25:06,100
That alone seems pretty impressive.

501
00:25:06,101 --> 00:25:07,400
Where to next?

502
00:25:07,401 --> 00:25:09,980
Well, there's a lot more to see, so let's move on.

503
00:25:09,981 --> 00:25:10,681
Lina.

504
00:25:10,682 --> 00:25:11,480
All right.

505
00:25:11,481 --> 00:25:12,680
You're doing excellent work.

506
00:25:12,681 --> 00:25:14,480
You're doing the Lord's work right now.

507
00:25:16,980 --> 00:25:19,320
Sometimes names can be deceiving.

508
00:25:19,321 --> 00:25:23,280
Just because a famous dish has a particular place name in it

509
00:25:23,281 --> 00:25:25,120
doesn't mean it was invented there.

510
00:25:25,121 --> 00:25:28,300
The prominence of some of our favorite foods

511
00:25:28,301 --> 00:25:30,160
that we think are from elsewhere

512
00:25:30,161 --> 00:25:32,860
are actually American inventions.

513
00:25:32,861 --> 00:25:36,360
English muffins were not created in England.

514
00:25:36,460 --> 00:25:40,640
They were invented in New York by baker Samuel Bath Thomas.

515
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:43,200
He debuted his toaster crumpets with their beloved

516
00:25:43,300 --> 00:25:46,740
nooks and crannies in 1880 and dubbed them

517
00:25:46,840 --> 00:25:49,080
English muffins years later.

518
00:25:49,180 --> 00:25:52,920
The Cuban sandwich must have been born in Cuba, right?

519
00:25:53,020 --> 00:25:53,820
Nope.

520
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:55,380
Florida.

521
00:25:55,480 --> 00:25:58,760
By 1900, the Cubano sandwich was well established

522
00:25:58,860 --> 00:26:01,420
as an inexpensive lunch for Cuban laborers

523
00:26:01,421 --> 00:26:03,120
in Florida's cigar factories.

524
00:26:03,220 --> 00:26:05,320
Tampa and Miami have been at odds

525
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:07,860
over who should be recognized as the true creator

526
00:26:07,960 --> 00:26:10,600
of the Cuban sandwich, with the Tampa City Council

527
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:14,460
even introducing a resolution with the exact way

528
00:26:14,560 --> 00:26:18,000
to make a historic Tampa Cuban sandwich,

529
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:21,900
down to using precisely three pickles.

530
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,680
But there is no mistaking the home country

531
00:26:24,780 --> 00:26:26,840
of this next food invention.

532
00:26:26,940 --> 00:26:30,280
Ranch dressing is so popular and so ubiquitous to America

533
00:26:30,281 --> 00:26:32,980
that in other parts of the world, ranch flavored foods

534
00:26:33,080 --> 00:26:34,980
are sometimes called Cool American.

535
00:26:35,080 --> 00:26:36,980
Ranch is a buttermilk-based dressing

536
00:26:37,080 --> 00:26:38,880
with dried herbs and spices.

537
00:26:38,980 --> 00:26:41,320
And with its versatile creamy tang,

538
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:44,320
it completely reinvented the whole idea

539
00:26:44,420 --> 00:26:46,560
of what a salad dressing could be.

540
00:26:46,660 --> 00:26:49,060
Before ranch came along, traditional dressings

541
00:26:49,160 --> 00:26:51,360
stayed pretty much where they were designed to be,

542
00:26:51,460 --> 00:26:52,700
on top of salad.

543
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:55,600
But from the beginning, ranch was different.

544
00:26:55,700 --> 00:26:58,380
It swiftly moved far beyond the salad bowl,

545
00:26:58,381 --> 00:27:01,140
becoming a dip, a sauce, a relish,

546
00:27:01,141 --> 00:27:03,880
even a popular flavor of Doritos.

547
00:27:03,881 --> 00:27:07,720
Ranch has become the number one favorite dressing

548
00:27:07,721 --> 00:27:08,720
for Americans.

549
00:27:08,721 --> 00:27:11,620
It has become a dip for fried food.

550
00:27:11,621 --> 00:27:13,860
It has become a topping for pizza.

551
00:27:13,861 --> 00:27:16,700
There's really nothing that people will not

552
00:27:16,701 --> 00:27:18,240
add some ranch dressing to.

553
00:27:18,241 --> 00:27:22,000
Ranch got its start in Alaska in the early 1950s

554
00:27:22,001 --> 00:27:24,540
when a Nebraskan named Steve Henson

555
00:27:24,541 --> 00:27:27,340
started mixing up his own buttermilk salad dressing

556
00:27:27,341 --> 00:27:28,880
for his fellow oil workers.

557
00:27:29,380 --> 00:27:31,080
Fresh herbs were hard to come by,

558
00:27:31,240 --> 00:27:34,040
so instead he added dried garlic, onion, and herbs

559
00:27:34,041 --> 00:27:35,000
to spice it up.

560
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:37,320
The dressing was a hit,

561
00:27:37,500 --> 00:27:39,120
and Steve took his recipe with him

562
00:27:39,121 --> 00:27:40,880
when he and his wife left Alaska

563
00:27:40,881 --> 00:27:43,720
and bought a guest ranch in the hills above Santa Barbara.

564
00:27:44,060 --> 00:27:46,340
They dubbed the ranch Hidden Valley.

565
00:27:46,720 --> 00:27:49,280
And here is where ranch dressing

566
00:27:49,281 --> 00:27:51,880
started to venture beyond the salad bowl,

567
00:27:52,180 --> 00:27:54,100
with Henson serving it on steaks,

568
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:55,920
potatoes, and vegetables.

569
00:27:55,921 --> 00:28:01,220
In fact, guests loved Steve's signature ranch dressing so much,

570
00:28:01,300 --> 00:28:03,820
they soon demanded to have it at home.

571
00:28:04,220 --> 00:28:07,500
But shipping a buttermilk-based dressing was impractical.

572
00:28:07,760 --> 00:28:12,000
So Steve created a spice packet of just the dry ingredients

573
00:28:12,001 --> 00:28:14,540
and mailed it to customers all around the country

574
00:28:14,541 --> 00:28:17,000
to mix with milk and mayonnaise.

575
00:28:17,340 --> 00:28:19,880
Clorox later bought Hidden Valley Ranch

576
00:28:19,881 --> 00:28:24,040
and finally developed a bottled version in the early 80s.

577
00:28:24,041 --> 00:28:27,380
Ranch is a distinctly American invention.

578
00:28:27,480 --> 00:28:29,440
