622
00:30:25,300 --> 00:30:27,300
Probably the most ancient problem

623
00:30:27,301 --> 00:30:29,300
when it comes to food is simply this,

624
00:30:29,301 --> 00:30:31,300
how to prevent it from spoiling.

625
00:30:31,301 --> 00:30:34,300
In recent centuries, two big innovations

626
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:36,300
have made it possible to preserve food

627
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:38,300
far longer than ever before.

628
00:30:38,301 --> 00:30:42,300
One was refrigeration, and the other canning.

629
00:30:42,301 --> 00:30:45,300
The idea of canning food actually goes back

630
00:30:45,301 --> 00:30:48,300
to the Napoleonic Wars, when the French government

631
00:30:48,301 --> 00:30:50,800
when the French government offered a prize to anyone

632
00:30:50,900 --> 00:30:53,300
who could figure out a way to preserve food

633
00:30:53,400 --> 00:30:55,040
for their large armies.

634
00:30:55,140 --> 00:30:57,740
A brewer named Nicolas Appert

635
00:30:57,840 --> 00:31:00,740
finally won the prize with a brand new technique.

636
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:04,020
He sealed food in an airtight glass jar

637
00:31:04,120 --> 00:31:07,180
and then dumped the jar into boiling water.

638
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:09,780
Now both protected from air and cooked,

639
00:31:09,880 --> 00:31:12,900
the contents of the jar were prevented from spoiling.

640
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:16,200
But if there's one name that is almost synonymous

641
00:31:16,201 --> 00:31:18,840
with canned foods in the American mind today,

642
00:31:18,940 --> 00:31:21,540
it's this one, Campbell's soup.

643
00:31:26,240 --> 00:31:28,240
Campbell's soup, it's iconic.

644
00:31:28,340 --> 00:31:30,540
It's something that you see in art, of course,

645
00:31:30,640 --> 00:31:33,360
and it's something that you see at the grocery store,

646
00:31:33,460 --> 00:31:35,060
and it's something from childhood.

647
00:31:35,160 --> 00:31:39,460
And it became kind of a cultural staple of American cuisine

648
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:42,300
in a way that is very uniquely American.

649
00:31:42,400 --> 00:31:44,160
In the late 19th century,

650
00:31:44,161 --> 00:31:46,360
America's soup makers had a problem.

651
00:31:46,460 --> 00:31:48,460
Soups were inexpensive to make,

652
00:31:48,560 --> 00:31:50,960
but very expensive to ship.

653
00:31:51,060 --> 00:31:53,160
Then, in 1897,

654
00:31:53,260 --> 00:31:55,500
Campbell's lead chemist John Dorrance

655
00:31:55,600 --> 00:31:57,100
came up with an innovation

656
00:31:57,200 --> 00:31:59,000
that would change the canning industry,

657
00:31:59,100 --> 00:32:02,120
as well as the American pantry, forever.

658
00:32:02,220 --> 00:32:05,420
He developed a way to remove the water from soup,

659
00:32:05,520 --> 00:32:08,520
while still retaining its essential ingredients and flavors.

660
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:13,820
Campbell's then sold it in a smaller, condensed soup can.

661
00:32:13,821 --> 00:32:16,660
Basically, that made it much less expensive to ship

662
00:32:16,760 --> 00:32:19,120
and much less expensive for the consumer.

663
00:32:19,220 --> 00:32:22,120
And that's kind of how it became very popular.

664
00:32:22,220 --> 00:32:25,560
You would add the water to the soup and heat it up.

665
00:32:25,660 --> 00:32:27,460
Soup used to cost 30 cents a can,

666
00:32:27,560 --> 00:32:30,060
and it knocked the price down to 10 cents a can

667
00:32:30,160 --> 00:32:33,180
and made it very profitable for Campbell's.

668
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:35,080
And Campbell's soup is still a staple

669
00:32:35,180 --> 00:32:37,580
of the American kitchen today, to the tune

670
00:32:37,680 --> 00:32:42,180
of over 1 billion cans sold every year.

